00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:02.639 align:start position:0%
today marks 100 years since one of the  worst  acts of racial violence in our country's  history the black  wall street massacre in tulsa oklahoma  between may 31st  and june 1st of 1921 tulsa's prosperous  african-american community known as  black wall street was attacked  planes even flew overhead dropping  explosives in a matter of hours the area  was demolished  more than 1200 homes and businesses were  destroyed  the vernon ame church is the only  building that survived the massacre  the original basement is still intact in  fact and it's where people hid from the  white mob  if you can imagine just being in here  100 years ago hearing the bombs go off  hearing women crying baby screaming and  you not knowing of your neck  hundreds were killed they were dumped in  mass graves  survivors rounded up and taken to  internment camps then deputized  by as white men by white men at beggar  pardon  later survivors filed more than a  million dollars in insurance claims and  they were all denied  no one was ever prosecuted or punished  for the massacre  tonight at 10 o'clock cbs this morning  anchor gayle king is hosting tulsa 1921  in american tragedy  that'll be right here on channel 8. the  special features  stories from survivors descendants and  historians well worth watching
 

